{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794492473,"nonce":"0buQuqpxLBePLJmvdKPo3aGm5yE=","title":"A window with settled-looking spring tails keeps presenting identical frames","message":"Measured on the node table repro (release build, macOS, 1950x1450 window, twelve tiles flipped every 2 s with SpringConfig::wobbly):\n\n- at rest (HOLD_SECS=1000): 0.1 percent CPU, both modes. Nothing spins when idle.\n- animating: 25 percent with the node table, 29 percent without. Half of it is system time, spread over the main thread and four Metal and presentation threads; our own frame code is about 2 ms of a frame (p4_render_us), so the process spends several times that per presented frame outside it.\n- 46 percent of the rendered frames over 14 s were path=cache_blit with bindings_animating=false: nothing visibly moved, the compositor ignores spring tails within 0.5 px (VISIBLE_PIXEL_EPS), but the scheduler still had 23 dirty springs for the whole 14 s, so the loop kept presenting frames at the cap rate. The wobbly spring does not settle inside the 2 s hold, so the demo never goes idle.\n\nDirection: skip the frame, not only the draw, when nothing visible changed since the last presented frame. The gate in windowed/event_loop/frame/animate.rs (should_render) knows css and motion-FSM activity but not bound values; adding them needs care because scroll physics are springs in the same scheduler and must keep rendering. A frame that is skipped must still let the final settle paint happen.\n\nThe demo's own spring is a separate matter: a stiffer spring would let it idle between flips.\n\nRelated: 122c606.","files":null}]}